rname{sec}⁡t.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Squaring the $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t$ term in the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity ${tan}^{2}⁡t+1={sec}^{2}⁡t$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {x}^{2}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-{x}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-89778/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89778"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-91322/selection/1998a883e5bd8ebda615ee65510e206c6636c59a45441f2cf5aea3b356456f6e" :answer/id #uuid "c2501f99-90b6-5a90-9b00-d0a071839134" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-91322/selection/cf4007c3fa4151e57698e948ec1fea6bf9b3de5d146d298803d0412645d0248d" :answer/id #uuid "aa6607c6-94e6-5756-ae9a-0a254d3bf75f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-91322/selection/d744d9ab3183cf3837867800b211357be827040da584c1e378e25939d8c6a6f5" :answer/id #uuid "3cd2afc3-8ad0-52fd-9616-d1f4e6630f97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-91322/selection/8bc45ce14dcb4287435e580f6557008264b2ada25586251f5397d94771419e1f" :answer/id #uuid "d7c9a6e1-ebf2-5344-9823-1907ded81afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (-\\pi ,0),\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-91322/selection/1be01a38655c9b80c5143f8e1af887b96d6afac0d8b99aa5a70070b461be0f30" :answer/id #uuid "215b54d5-ee02-5271-9669-ad4be828308d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-91322/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fcd964d-0e31-56ad-b64e-f9ba5be6135d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-91322/selection/1998a883e5bd8ebda615ee65510e206c6636c59a45441f2cf5aea3b356456f6e" :answer-field/choices ["q-91322/selection/1998a883e5bd8ebda615ee65510e206c6636c59a45441f2cf5aea3b356456f6e" "q-91322/selection/cf4007c3fa4151e57698e948ec1fea6bf9b3de5d146d298803d0412645d0248d" "q-91322/selection/d744d9ab3183cf3837867800b211357be827040da584c1e378e25939d8c6a6f5" "q-91322/selection/8bc45ce14dcb4287435e580f6557008264b2ada25586251f5397d94771419e1f" "q-91322/selection/1be01a38655c9b80c5143f8e1af887b96d6afac0d8b99aa5a70070b461be0f30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-91322" :question/id #uuid "29abe703-1b84-5d51-a4f6-f49a9a4dc183" :question/math-academy-id "q-91322" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/82bdf962acd0e4a9beb29c7f3cc4930e66e6e56f41f81ba944fd470edfdb2cd8.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ec38ac9d02fbc8ec8c4f79fccb36a0e91833e852127fd1a8c84e492276b8f95d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-91322/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-91322"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-106682/selection/b85ba2ba54d596653a563476b4f78775ae06d7693e7e2bfe7b1bde461b8c1155" :answer/id #uuid "b5149c80-0510-5472-b9a6-0e2b784675fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106682/selection/7815766c3d40aa5830f35a4589f31a583d578ff12d207e41785c4ae03823a742" :answer/id #uuid "de94a279-5292-51ae-b81b-55109917d031" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,0],\\,t\\ne -\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106682/selection/cc6f942a79e53dd750bb319cae85dc163741c466afba61e244e0fa263b5fe331" :answer/id #uuid "5d2bee71-093b-5afb-8894-bc126721dc12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106682/selection/45cd2b735740b382dba8c4a9519aef5f8524ea3401f4c7f61b59893918513571" :answer/id #uuid "9d44f8a8-171a-5f9a-b253-dea04c59a2e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106682/selection/11ea745910ff697b9055ddbb7e7b8a23d1d92eec0a8c07053d7df7aa989d1cf5" :answer/id #uuid "df3a649c-124d-535c-a595-7ddf961707a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-106682/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "161b36d7-587e-5c40-a2b5-a00cf3315381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-106682/selection/b85ba2ba54d596653a563476b4f78775ae06d7693e7e2bfe7b1bde461b8c1155" :answer-field/choices ["q-106682/selection/b85ba2ba54d596653a563476b4f78775ae06d7693e7e2bfe7b1bde461b8c1155" "q-106682/selection/7815766c3d40aa5830f35a4589f31a583d578ff12d207e41785c4ae03823a742" "q-106682/selection/cc6f942a79e53dd750bb319cae85dc163741c466afba61e244e0fa263b5fe331" "q-106682/selection/45cd2b735740b382dba8c4a9519aef5f8524ea3401f4c7f61b59893918513571" "q-106682/selection/11ea745910ff697b9055ddbb7e7b8a23d1d92eec0a8c07053d7df7aa989d1cf5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106682" :question/id #uuid "5fe995fe-d9fb-57f1-81bc-8d7ca13c35e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-106682" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ab11a3dde0aebbd4088c498e37da48909f9a309ca9d4ab29bfcfa788d53e5c5e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/f825ca9f9431ca38b14ed141639d5cf4189f0fde0526e943ddb956e112c3e4ab.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106682/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106682"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-90951"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/600981b59a97fd1d63c9da1f7ef45d58c8a2d5dcad1ec05df91a380e0f4e154e.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/747613184fe04ecd53fb69e6e00c5d91152e571e9bc4db81adeef1063c057491.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ],$ we get only the lower branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the lower branch are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-326334/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer/id #uuid "dae80fb9-5c18-57b5-b4c9-82cebb514975" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326334/field-1/93b23be7eccffafc428741fe46d5e1a8a45d9a44730ec29ee6c035137bd18fed" :answer/id #uuid "de67c31e-a563-5e0e-8ba9-590a5dd6ccad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326334/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "264d09be-01e6-51cb-8336-7f9cefe6c971" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-326334/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-326334/field-1/0e4e79001619645600fe317a8a7dc3e7c54e40dedca3eec2190744766458f0b4" "q-326334/field-1/93b23be7eccffafc428741fe46d5e1a8a45d9a44730ec29ee6c035137bd18fed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-326334/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer/id #uuid "e5bd24f6-6d6e-535e-b32c-a9d04c9158b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-326334/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b997d0-94ec-5723-916d-f0d7431121d4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-326334/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77" :answer-field/choices ["q-326334/field-2/e7e505d325c3cadeeeaf26a0afda20aaa10bea508f9739931632d1057683cd77"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-326334" :question/id #uuid "41eceb87-fa62-57cc-85ca-797ce60afd1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-326334" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/28c189efc805ec6aef299c4a89c1bb9299db7ad9f8544b86ed7509e6abc2e6a5.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this branch, assuming that the entire hyperbola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b8b14a6d9bf2f77c79b26172ec18e7833c1315ccb09c94be5a05b7e12714a14f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-326334/field-1" "q-326334/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-326334"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-91323"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/7be6e3cc933586287cabf590d2a3c5fede604b6c4222a39c9db78f3a3745f814.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ebb0f765fca0bc4e3a0a789800779a51eb4173cf0384b8b3628ac679a5a3d692.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7886"] :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ and $b = \\sqrt{25} = 5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7884"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ac922b185c42645f703c13d4f5016941b4125d81e85fa5ff529aa5c8d4010ac8.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x.$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are $y = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y = \\pm 3 x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{b} & =3 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7887"] :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8055"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/2944e095d71b64fb2f9e6e0121a09fb9eb2f3ffe765f9881162c95fe6875a831.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b1d78a939f85c533bcd9b4609346f2a15385aad3a6b074a62b791551cf784418.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
